# The ledger of `redis`: what a program does with a Redis server, measured
# against Go's `github.com/redis/go-redis/v9` v9.23.0 (its `go doc -all`, the
# module fetched into the scratch folder on 2026-11-18, every method of its
# `*Cmdable` interfaces) and Python's redis-py 8.1.0 (`dir(redis.Redis)`,
# `redis.client.Pipeline`, `redis.client.PubSub` and `redis.lock.Lock`,
# installed into the scratch folder the same day). Rust's standard library
# has no Redis client, so its line is `-` in every block.
#
# Both libraries give a typed function to each of Redis's commands; here a
# command is a list of arguments (`call`, `call_bytes`) and its reply a
# `Reply`, read by `text_of`, `integer_of`, `float_of`, `boolean_of`,
# `list_of`, `texts_of` and `entries_of`, which read RESP2's and RESP3's
# answers alike. So a family of commands is one capability, done by `call`;
# its typed function in Go and Python is named in its block, and a typed
# record of one command's answer is dropped below. A handful of the commonest
# have a function of their own (`get`, `set`, `set_for`, `delete`,
# `incr_by`, `publish`).
#
# Every expected value of the tests was written from Redis's "Protocol
# specification" (RESP2 and RESP3) and "Redis serialization protocol" pages:
# the bytes of a command, of each type of reply, of an attribute, of a push,
# and the confirmations of a subscription. The live run, on 2026-11-18,
# reached Redis 8.0.2 in a container of this Mac, in plain text and over
# TLS (the certificates of `tls`'s tests), RESP3 and RESP2.

capability redis/open  a connection opened, logged in, its protocol agreed and its database selected
  heroes  redis.open, redis.options, redis.Options, redis.Client, redis.DEFAULT_PORT
  go      NewClient, Options, Client, Conn, Client.Conn, Client.Options, Options.NewDialer, NewDialer
  rust    -
  python  Redis, StrictRedis, Connection, from_pool
  note    one connection, which the program owes a `close`; a pool is refused below

capability redis/url  a connection's options read from a `redis://` or `rediss://` URL
  heroes  redis.parse_url
  go      ParseURL
  rust    -
  python  from_url
  note    its path is the database; `protocol` and `client_name` read from its query; `unix://` fails `unsupported`

capability redis/close  a connection closed, and asked whether it is
  heroes  redis.close, redis.is_closed
  go      Client.Close, Conn.Close
  rust    -
  python  close
  note    the socket closed; Redis 7.2 deprecates `QUIT`, so none is sent

capability redis/protocol  RESP3 asked for, RESP2 where the server knows no HELLO
  heroes  redis.open, redis.protocol_of
  go      Options (Protocol)
  rust    -
  python  Redis (protocol)
  note    `HELLO 3` with the login and the name in it; go-redis falls back the same way, redis-py asks RESP2 unless told

capability redis/login  a password and a user of Redis 6's access lists sent at opening
  heroes  redis.open, redis.Options
  go      Options (Username, Password, CredentialsProvider), Auth, AuthACL, Select, SwapDB, ClientSetName, ClientSetInfo, Hello
  rust    -
  python  auth, credentials, CredentialProvider, UsernamePasswordCredentialProvider, auth, client_setinfo, client_setname, hello, select, swapdb, reset
  note    `HELLO 3 AUTH user password`, or `AUTH` over RESP2; `SELECT` and `CLIENT SETNAME` after it; `SWAPDB` and `RESET` run through `call`. A server started with no password, or whose user is `nopass`, takes any password in `HELLO 3 AUTH` (Redis 8.10.2, run 2026-11-19), so the live test asks the server first, `AUTH <user> <a wrong password>` on its open connection, and asserts that `open` with a wrong password fails `permission` only where the server refused that `AUTH`; elsewhere it prints why it did not try. Until that day it asserted the failure on every server and failed on one with no password (the jobs agent's report): held on both kinds that day, plain and `--sanitize`, 1201 tests passing each time

capability redis/tls  TLS from the first byte, the server's certificate checked for its authority and its host
  heroes  redis.Security, redis.open, redis.parse_url
  go      Options (TLSConfig)
  rust    -
  python  SSLConnection
  note    `rediss://`; through `tls.connect`, so on macOS, Linux and Windows alike

capability redis/tls-client-certificate  a client's own certificate presented to the server
  heroes  pending
  go      Options (TLSConfig)
  rust    -
  python  Redis (ssl_certfile, ssl_keyfile)
  note    `tls.Client` holds no certificate of the client's; Redis asks for one where `tls-auth-clients` is on

capability redis/deadline  every wait on a connection bounded in time, and the bound moved
  heroes  redis.Options, redis.set_deadline
  go      Options (DialTimeout, ReadTimeout, WriteTimeout), Client.WithTimeout
  rust    -
  python  Redis (socket_timeout, socket_connect_timeout)
  note    one deadline from the connection on, moved by `set_deadline`, which bounds a `receive`

capability redis/command  any command run, its arguments a list never joined into text
  heroes  redis.call, redis.call_bytes, redis.send, redis.read_reply, redis.checked
  go      Client.Do, Client.Process, Client.DoRaw, Conn.Do, Conn.Process, Conn.DoRaw, Cmd, NewCmd, ExtractCommandValue
  rust    -
  python  execute_command
  note    an error reply fails with its code from `call`, and comes as `.error` from `read_reply`, which `checked` turns into a failure

capability redis/replies  a reply read into a variant of RESP3's types, and read as text, bytes, numbers, truths, lists and maps
  heroes  redis.Reply, redis.Entry, redis.text_of, redis.bytes_of, redis.integer_of, redis.float_of, redis.boolean_of, redis.list_of, redis.texts_of, redis.entries_of, redis.is_null, redis.kind_of
  go      Cmd.Result, Cmd.Text, Cmd.Int64, Cmd.Float64, Cmd.Bool, Cmd.Slice, Nil
  rust    -
  python  parse_response, set_response_callback
  note    the readers read RESP2's strings of digits and flat arrays as RESP3's numbers and maps; a null is `not_found`, where Go answers `redis.Nil`

capability redis/decode  the bytes of a reply read without a connection, and a command written as bytes
  heroes  redis.decode, redis.decode_next, redis.Decoded, redis.encode_command
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    its own stack, a reply nested past 128 aggregates refused; an attribute (`|`) read and dropped

